From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mark Chiou <chiouss@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 18:52:22 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] qualcommax: ipq50xx: add support for Zyxel WAX640S-6E

The Zyxel WAX640S-6E is a tri-band 802.11ax (Wi-Fi 6E) access point
based on the Qualcomm AP-MP03.3 reference design. It shares the flash
layout, Ethernet and port LEDs of the NWA220AX-6E (common
ipq5018-zyxel-6e.dtsi), but has a QCN6102 for 5 GHz next to the
QCN9024, which is used for 6 GHz only.

Hardware
--------
SoC:    Qualcomm IPQ5018
RAM:    1G DDR3
FLASH:  16M SPI-NOR (Macronix MX25U12835F)
        256M SPI-NAND (Macronix MX35UF2GE4AD)
ETH:    1x 2.5 Gigabit Ethernet (QCA8081, PoE-in, "uplink")
        1x Gigabit Ethernet (IPQ5018 internal PHY, "lan1")
WiFi:   Qualcomm IPQ5018 (2.4GHz 802.11ax, 2x2)
        Qualcomm QCN6102 (5GHz 802.11ax, 2x2)
        Qualcomm QCN9024 (6GHz 802.11ax, 4x4)
BT:     Qualcomm IPQ5018 (BT 5.1, not supported)
BTN:    1x Reset
LED:    1x Multi-Color (RGBW, TI LP5562)
        2x port LEDs on each Ethernet port
Power:  802.3af/at/bt PoE or 12V/2.5A DC

UART Console
------------
4-pin console header on the board.

Settings: 3.3V TTL, 115200 8N1

Installation Web-UI
-------------------
The device uses a dual-image setup and OpenWrt can only be installed as
image 0, as the bootloader always passes "ubi.mtd=rootfs" to the kernel.
When the currently running stock firmware is image 0, OpenWrt will be
installed as image 1, fail to boot and the device returns to stock
firmware. If this happens, install any version of stock firmware so
that it runs as image 1, before installing OpenWrt. Alternatively, if
there already is a valid stock firmware in image 1, the "debug
dual-image show" and "debug dual-image set boot-image image1" commands
can be used in the stock CLI via serial/SSH/telnet to switch to image 1.

 * Rename the OpenWrt factory image to a shorter name, for example
   "openwrt.bin" (the stock firmware has a character limit)
 * In the web interface, go to "Maintenance" -> "File Manager" ->
   "Firmware Package" (or click the link next to "Firmware Version"
   under "Device Information" on the dashboard)
 * Under "Upload File" browse to the renamed OpenWrt factory image and
   click on "Upload"

The factory image is a UBI image wrapped in an outer FIT carrying the
device model ID ("74 e1"), which the stock firmware checks before
accepting it. The stock firmware flashes the "ubi" section into the
inactive image. On first boot, zyxel-bootconfig marks the OpenWrt image
as the selected one so the bootloader keeps booting it.

Switch Boot Image
-----------------
OpenWrt to stock: "zyxel-bootconfig-ipq set image1"
Stock to OpenWrt: "debug dual-image set boot-image image0"
In the bootloader: "atgo 0" / "atgo 1"

Unbrick / Revert to Stock with the Boot Module
----------------------------------------------
 * Disconnect the device from power
 * Configure your machine to 192.168.1.103/24 and start a TFTP server
 * Put the stock firmware image into the TFTP server root and rename it
   to "ZLD-current"
 * Establish a serial connection to the device through the console port
 * Connect the device to power
 * When prompted, press a key to abort automatic boot and enter debug
   mode
 * Use the "atnz" command to flash the firmware image
 * Use the "atgo" command to boot from the newly flashed image

Flash layout
------------
Same as the NWA220AX-6E: 0:TRAINING, rootfs (image 0), rootfs_1
(image 1), rootfs_data, myzyxel and logs, as reported by the stock
kernel. The stock rootfs_data partition is named zyxel_rootfs_data to
not be picked up as the OpenWrt overlay.

MAC addresses
-------------
uplink:     base MAC (mrd partition, offset 0xfff8), matches the label
lan1:       base MAC + 1
WiFi 2.4G:  base MAC + 2
WiFi 5G:    base MAC + 3
WiFi 6G:    base MAC + 4

Ethernet
--------
Same as the NWA220AX-6E, on the NSS-DP/SSDK stack.

LEDs
----
The status LED is driven by a TI LP5562 on I2C.
The port LEDs work as on the NWA220AX-6E: the uplink LEDs are driven by
the QCA8081 (green at 2.5G, amber at 10/100/1000), the lan1 LEDs by the
IPQ5018 internal PHY through GPIO46 (green at 1G) and GPIO30 (amber at
10/100). Both blink on traffic while lit.

WiFi
----
The board data (calibration variant "Zyxel-WAX640S-6E") comes from
the ipq-wifi-zyxel_wax640s-6e package in the zyxel feed.

The QCN6102 is handled by the ath11k QCN6122 support (user PD 2, reset
GPIO18 passed to the Q6 firmware), the QCN9024 by the ath11k QCN9074
support. The calibration data of the three radios is in 0:art at
0x1000 (IPQ5018), 0x26800 (QCN6102) and 0x4c000 (QCN9024).
The QCN6102 signals its interrupts as fixed SPIs of a GICv2m frame.
With the default first frame they overlapped the MSIs the kernel hands
out to the PCIe bus of the QCN9024 from the same frame: the PCIe root
port shared an interrupt with the QCN6102 and a reboot warned about a
leaked "ce0" handler. Use the second frame for the QCN6102, as the
second QCN6122 instance in ipq5018-qcn6122.dtsi does. The radio then
appears as b00b040.wifi, so its calibration data is extracted for that
name and an existing wireless config is migrated to the new path.
The antenna selection GPIOs are held at the level the stock firmware
sets up at boot; its smart antenna switching is not supported.
6 GHz requires a country code and WPA3-SAE. Most clients only scan the
6 GHz preferred scanning channels (PSC: 5, 21, 37, ...), so choose one
of them instead of relying on ACS.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: Mark Chiou <chiouss@gmail.com>
